<% content_for :title, "Help — Informed Opinion" %>

<section class="help-page ui-page">
  <div class="container help-container">
    <header class="ui-masthead">
      <p class="ui-kicker">Using the site</p>
      <h1>Help</h1>
      <p>How to explore a question, learn from its evidence and understand what your answers—and the public results—mean.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="help-layout">
      <nav class="help-contents ui-panel" aria-label="On this page">
        <strong>On this page</strong>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#start">A useful way to start</a></li>
          <li><a href="#question">Read the proposition</a></li>
          <li><a href="#facts">Use the fact questions</a></li>
          <li><a href="#progress">Your view and progress</a></li>
          <li><a href="#results">Read public results</a></li>
          <li><a href="#trust">Check and challenge</a></li>
          <li><a href="#contributing">Propose a question</a></li>
          <li><a href="#limits">Keep the limits in mind</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <article class="help-article">
        <section id="start">
          <p class="ui-kicker">Getting started</p>
          <h2>You can browse before deciding whether to take part</h2>
          <p>Open any topic to see its exact proposition, the current result and the number of people behind it. You do not need an account to browse.</p>
          <p>If a question interests you, sign in to record your view and try its fact questions. There is no need to finish in one visit, and you can change your view later.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="question">
          <p class="ui-kicker">Before answering</p>
          <h2>Read the proposition, not only the title</h2>
          <p>The title is a short label. The proposition underneath defines what you are being asked to judge—often including a place, policy or period. Two people may agree about the subject but answer differently because they interpret the proposed action differently.</p>
          <p>Choose the response closest to your present view. It is fine to be unsure or neutral; the point is to record where you stand before and after seeing the evidence.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="facts">
          <p class="ui-kicker">Learning</p>
          <h2>Treat the quiz as a guided tour of the evidence</h2>
          <p>Each question tests a fact that may help someone judge the proposition. After answering, you see the correct answer, a short explanation and a link to the source. Following a source is worthwhile when a figure surprises you or a qualification matters to your view.</p>
          <p>The opening questions usually put the main issues on the table quickly. Later questions may introduce uncertainty, limits and evidence that challenges your starting position. Every question stands alone, so you can stop and resume without losing the thread.</p>
          <p>A wrong answer is not a penalty or a verdict on your judgement. It identifies something useful to learn, and only your latest answer to that fact counts.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="progress">
          <p class="ui-kicker">Your account</p>
          <h2>Your view, answers and weight can change</h2>
          <p>Your opinions page shows the topics you have joined, your current position and your progress through the facts. You may revise an opinion whenever your judgement changes.</p>
          <p>Correct answers give that opinion knowledge weight. More consequential facts count somewhat more than supporting details, but the range is deliberately small. Weight belongs only to that topic: doing well on one subject does not give your opinions extra influence elsewhere.</p>
          <p>Your individual view and answers are not displayed publicly. They contribute to the combined result without being attached to your name.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="results">
          <p class="ui-kicker">Browsing results</p>
          <h2>Look at direction, strength and participation</h2>
          <p>Each result runs from <strong>No</strong>, through the middle, to <strong>Yes</strong>. The marker combines participants' positions while giving more influence to opinions backed by more correct answers on that topic.</p>
          <p>Always look at the participant counts as well as the marker. A strong-looking result based on a few people is fragile. A result near the middle may mean that participants are neutral, divided, or that strongly held views balance one another.</p>
          <p>The site also shows ordinary accuracy alongside weighted accuracy so you can see how much the editorial importance ratings affect a score. The exact calculation is available on the <%= link_to "methodology page", methodology_path %>.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="trust">
          <p class="ui-kicker">Checking the material</p>
          <h2>Sources and corrections are part of the experience</h2>
          <p>Fact questions link to the evidence supporting their answers. Explanations should distinguish what the source establishes from what remains uncertain or a matter of values.</p>
          <p>If something appears inaccurate, unclear, outdated or irrelevant, use the report option on that fact. Moderators review reports and can correct or retire questions. You can also give feedback on whether an opinion question itself is useful.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="contributing">
          <p class="ui-kicker">Contributing</p>
          <h2>Propose a question you think the site should examine</h2>
          <p>The <%= link_to "Proposals page", opinion_question_proposals_path %> shows the complete submission form to everyone. A useful proposal is one clear, genuinely disputed proposition whose underlying facts could influence people's views.</p>
          <p>You need an account to send it, but you can draft it before signing in. Moderators may refine the wording, research a fact bank and publish it only after the complete material has been reviewed.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="limits">
          <p class="ui-kicker">Reading with care</p>
          <h2>The result describes participants; it does not settle the issue</h2>
          <p>People choose whether to use the site, so its participants are not necessarily representative of the wider public. A high weight means success on the selected facts—not general expertise, sincerity, wisdom or moral authority.</p>
          <p>Facts can correct beliefs and clarify trade-offs, but they do not decide how much importance you should give competing values. The most useful question to take away is often not “Which side won?” but “Which evidence changed—or failed to change—my view, and why?”</p>
        </section>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
